so 2015's coming to a close and with it  the end of the year with a lot of  awesome games there were massive games  open world games really great rpgs  really great stealth games which leads  me to my number five metal gear solid 5  the phantom pain does a lot of things  well but it also does a lot of things  well that other games haven't really  done before because you can pretty much  just scout out of town from a mile away  in this open world but then when you  actually get into the town's it's just  really tense moment to moment gameplay  that the series is known for there's  also just an insane amount of options on  how you can tackle situations you can go  at it like a shooter with machine guns  and riot shields you can go at it just  like classic stealth which I prefer and  you know use the tranq darts and use  d-dog my favorite character to try to  pick out people as you're going through  towns but in general it just also told  this really great yet melodramatic story  that kind of drove home the idea of loss  and these really deep themes throughout  the entire experience my number 4 game  was rocket League I can't really pick  out another game this year that I've had  more fun with and every time I go back I  somehow keep having more fun and I can  really see me tangible effects of my  practice whether I'm playing online or  whether I'm playing here in the game  spot office or whether I'm teaching  someone at home like old friends you  haven't really played it before there's  such great mechanics wrapped around this  overall experience that I never really  was frustrated with even I was down like  six cold I still felt like there's  something I could be doing to help my  team there's something I could be doing  that I haven't really tried before leave  so much room for experimentation but at  the same time it's such a fine tuned  experience which brings us to number  three which for me was the witcher 3  wild hunt and this really is one of the  best RPGs I've ever played the world  itself is so fun to explore and its  massive and you really get a sense of  place as you're discovering these new  creatures that are really unique to just  this universe but also at the same time  this is the first time I can remember  where I wasn't just completing quests  for the experience and for the new gear  I was completing quests because the  stories were actually really interesting  and these characters were all convincing  and I really just wanted to see what  happened next as the plot unfolded the  structure itself was also really great  as you  approach this part where I know a lot of  people thought it was the end but it  turns out that was just a climate and  you have another few hours of falling  action as you really get to know a few  of the characters and my number 2 game  was rise of the Tomb Raider which really  surprised me I was expecting to like it  a lot because i love the 2013 reboot but  rise of the Tomb Raider capitalized on  so much of what made that reboot good it  really had to put it this high on the  list there's just really no fat on the  entire experience it's got the great  pacing of something like resident evil 4  it's got the quality kind of rewarding  backtracking of Metroid Prime and it's  also got these third person action  elements that coalesce so well it's got  great combats got exploration that leads  to this really in-depth crafting and in  the end the story really satisfied me  because it wasn't more of like these  random supernatural twists it was  something that actually sort of made  sense throughout the story that brings  me to my favorite game of 2015 and I  have to give it to blood-borne  no matter how many other games I played  throughout the year nothing was really  quite as satisfying his blood borne one  of the most rewarding experiences  despite how brutal it can be I remember  at least five times when I just had to  quit for the night because I couldn't  beat that boss I couldn't get past that  certain enemy I couldn't unlock the next  checkpoint it really was just the  learning experience the entire time and  now that I've gone back to it throughout  the year I really felt like I've gotten  better and despite that it keeps  punishing me and it's always just  different every time I play despite the  fact that I'm retreading these same  areas you could pick a new weapon it  will completely change how you play the  game and now that I've gone back to the  expansion with the old hunters I love it  even more and it's probably one of the  games I've gone back to throughout the  year more than any other  you
 

